NAMES.long:
This quantity is the geometric average of the reciprocal major radius over a complete magnetic flux surface. It is an equilibrium metric coefficient independent of plasma species, population, magnetic-field component, and physical transport process.

For a magnetic flux surface identified by the label $\psi$, the defining flux-surface average is:

$$
g_{1/R}(\psi) =
\left\langle \frac{1}{R} \right\rangle_\psi =
\frac{\displaystyle\oint_{\mathcal{S}(\psi)}
\frac{dS}{|\nabla\psi|}\,\frac{1}{R}}
{\displaystyle\oint_{\mathcal{S}(\psi)}
\frac{dS}{|\nabla\psi|}}
$$

Here $g_{1/R}$ denotes the metric coefficient, $R$ is the major-radius coordinate, $\psi$ is the magnetic flux-surface label, $\mathcal{S}(\psi)$ is the corresponding closed magnetic flux surface, $dS$ is its surface-area element, and $\nabla\psi$ is the spatial gradient of the flux-surface label. The coordinate $R$ belongs to the right-handed cylindrical $(R,\phi,Z)$ frame, where $\phi$ is the toroidal angle and $Z$ is the vertical coordinate.

The aggregation is over the entire closed flux surface using the flux-surface weighting shown in the defining relation; this is not the local reciprocal radius at a point. The quantity is distinct from the flux-surface average of reciprocal squared major radius, [flux-surface-averaged inverse square of major radius](name:flux_surface_averaged_inverse_of_square_of_major_radius).

It is the flux-surface-averaged specialization of [inverse major radius](name:inverse_of_major_radius): the averaging operator is applied to the local reciprocal-major-radius quantity without introducing a species, field-component, or process restriction.
